ion of both directory permissions and cross-user delete-protection behavior post-restart. Temporary verification artifacts (test users) were fully cleaned up, leaving no residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"The evidence set for the initial reboot poll shows a transient connection timeout (cmd-9ed630ce68774235) rather than an explicit down/unreachable confirmation, so the exact moment of shutdown is inferred rather than directly observed, though the subsequent boot_id change is conclusive.\",\"No SELinux policy adjustment was made or shown necessary; functional tests succeeded despite the var_t context, so this is not treated as a gap, but it was not exhaustively probed against all possible access patterns.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"/srv/drop exists as a shared, writable directory on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Directory was absent (cmd-01e74dfb518d427a), then created via mkdir/chown/chmod 1777 (cmd-0ca70b4680ce4a83), showing drwxrwxrwt root:root. Two independent unprivileged users (dropuser1, dropuser2) successfully wrote files into it (cmd-f659dcf410334c34, cmd-890cc87eb0d941d6), confirming shared writability. State persisted after the node1 restart (cmd-fb7fb5682c9d4ffd).","evidence_ids":["cmd-01e74dfb518d427a","cmd-0ca70b4680ce4a83","cmd-f659dcf410334c34","cmd-890cc87eb0d941d6","cmd-fb7fb5682c9d4ffd"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Users cannot remove files owned by other users in /srv/drop (sticky-bit protection)","status":"satisfied","summary":"With sticky bit set (mode 1777), dropuser2's attempt to delete dropuser1's file failed with 'Operation not permitted' (cmd-890cc87eb0d941d6), while each user could delete their own file (cmd-7e30cc1a53b14e2c). The same cross-delete-denied / own-delete-allowed behavior was re-demonstrated with fresh users after the node1 restart (cmd-d9f4178730204315, cmd-52078464b35243ae), directly proving the protection persists across a reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-890cc87eb0d941d6","cmd-7e30c